## Summary

On macOS, clicking the red traffic-light button does nothing — no close, no dialog, no error. `Cmd+Q` is equally dead. This survives an app restart, so it is not a transient stuck state.

The window becomes **permanently and silently unclosable** whenever the renderer's shutdown checkpoint fails to persist. The only way out is `pkill -x Orca`.

This is a sibling of #5144 (fixed for the `isQuitting` path in 1.4.56). The watchdog added there does not cover this case, for two separate reasons described below.

**Environment:** Orca 1.4.187 · macOS (Darwin 25.5.0, arm64) · Electron 43.1.0

## Root cause

`persist()` inside `createShutdownCheckpointGuard(...)` (`out/renderer/assets/App-*.js`) ends with an **unguarded** IPC call:

```js
const shutdownCheckpoint = createShutdownCheckpointGuard(() => {
  const shouldCaptureSession = shouldPersistWorkspaceSession(useAppStore.getState());
  // ...
  let sessionSnapshots = [];
  try {
    sessionSnapshots = shouldCaptureSession
      ? buildWorkspaceSessionHostSnapshots(buildWorkspaceSessionPayload(freshState), freshState)
      : [];
  } catch (error) {
    if (!isIntentionalAppRestartInProgress() || freshState.openFiles.some(f => f.isDirty)) throw error;
    console.error("[app] Full renderer session snapshot failed; using durable session", error);
    window.api.app.stageBeforeUnloadSync({ sessions: [], ui: buildActiveViewUnloadPatch(freshState) });
    return;                                     // graceful degradation exists...
  }
  window.api.app.stageBeforeUnloadSync({        // ...but THIS call is outside the try
    sessions: sessionSnapshots,
    ui: buildActiveViewUnloadPatch(freshState)
  });
});
```

When the main-process handler throws, the failure propagates all the way back into a `preventDefault()`:

```js
// out/main/index.js — app:stage-before-unload-sync
electron.ipcMain.on("app:stage-before-unload-sync", (event, args) => {
  let ok = true;
  try {
    for (const { state, hostId } of args.sessions) store.stageWorkspaceSessionBeforeUnload(state, hostId);
    store.updateUI(args.ui);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error("[app] Failed to stage renderer state before unload:", error);
    ok = false;
  }
  event.returnValue = { ok };
});
```

```js
// out/preload/index.js
stageBeforeUnloadSync: (args) => {
  if (electron.ipcRenderer.sendSync("app:stage-before-unload-sync", args)?.ok !== true)
    throw new Error("Failed to stage renderer state before unload.");
},
```

```js
// out/renderer/assets/shutdown-checkpoint-guard-*.js
function createShutdownCheckpointGuard(persist) {
  let persisted = false;
  return {
    persistOnce() {
      if (persisted) return true;
      try { persist(); } catch { return false; }   // exception swallowed, no logging
      persisted = true;
      return true;
    },
    reset() { persisted = false; }
  };
}

function createShutdownCheckpointBeforeUnloadHandler(guard) {
  return (event) => {
    if (!guard.persistOnce()) {
      event.currentTarget?.dispatchEvent(new Event(ORCA_RENDERER_SHUTDOWN_CHECKPOINT_FAILED_EVENT));
      event.preventDefault();                       // ← window refuses to close
    }
  };
}
```

Full chain:

```
red button
 └─ main: mainWindow.on("close") → e.preventDefault() → send "window:close-requested"
     └─ renderer: close guards → activeHandler → local PTY child-process check → "Close Window?" dialog
         └─ user confirms → confirmNativeWindowClose()
             └─ if (!window.dispatchEvent(new Event("beforeunload", {cancelable:true}))) return;
                 └─ shutdown-checkpoint handler → persist() → stageBeforeUnloadSync() throws
                     └─ persistOnce() → false → event.preventDefault()
                         └─ dispatchEvent() returns false → confirmNativeWindowClose() returns early,
                            never calling window.api.ui.confirmWindowClose()
```

And on a direct `mainWindow.close()` (e.g. after `window:confirm-close`), Chromium fires the real `beforeunload`, the same handler cancels it, and:

```js
mainWindow.webContents.on("will-prevent-unload", () => {
  windowClosing = false;
  clearQuitRendererAckTimer();
  opts?.onQuitAborted?.();
  mainWindow.webContents.send("window:unload-prevented");
  // ← no event.preventDefault(), so Electron honours the cancellation and the close is aborted
});
```

`window:unload-prevented` then calls `shutdownCheckpoint.reset()`, so the next attempt re-runs the same failing `persist()` and fails identically. **Permanent loop.**

## Runtime evidence

Collected from the renderer DevTools console on an affected instance:

| Probe | Result | Conclusion |
|---|---|---|
| `window.dispatchEvent(new Event("beforeunload",{cancelable:true}))` | `false` | a listener calls `preventDefault()` |
| listen for `orca:renderer-shutdown-checkpoint-failed` while dispatching | `checkpointFailed: true` | it is the shutdown-checkpoint handler; `persist()` threw |
| `window.api.app.stageBeforeUnloadSync({sessions:[],ui:{}})` | `"ok"` | the IPC channel and handler are healthy for an empty payload |
| `ipcRenderer.invoke("app:await-before-unload-checkpoint")` after a real `beforeunload` | `{ok:false}` | main **was** reached with the real payload and **did** throw |

I also dispatched `orca:app-restart-started` to force `isIntentionalAppRestartInProgress()` to `true`. The result was still `prevented: true` and the `"Full renderer session snapshot failed; using durable session"` line was **never logged** — confirming the throw happens at the final `stageBeforeUnloadSync` call, outside the `try` block, where the degradation path cannot reach it.

## Why the #5144 watchdog does not help

Two independent gaps:

**1. No watchdog at all on the non-quit path.** `armQuitRendererAckTimer(requestId)` is only armed when `isQuitting` is true:

```js
const isQuitting = opts?.getIsQuitting?.() ?? false;
const requestId = ++closeRequestSequence;
if (isQuitting) armQuitRendererAckTimer(requestId);      // ← clicking the red button is isQuitting=false
mainWindow.webContents.send("window:close-requested", { isQuitting, requestId });
```

**2. The ack proves delivery, not progress.** The preload acknowledges unconditionally *before* invoking the callback:

```js
onWindowCloseRequested: (callback) => {
  const listener = (_event, data) => {
    electron.ipcRenderer.send("window:close-request-received", data?.requestId);  // ← always acks
    callback({ isQuitting: data?.isQuitting ?? false });
  };
  // ...
}
```

So `onCloseRequestReceived` clears the timer even when the close flow subsequently stalls or aborts. The watchdog can only detect a dead/hung renderer, never a stalled close flow. This is why `Cmd+Q` is also permanently dead here.

## Why it is invisible

- `persistOnce()` uses a bare `catch { return false }` — the exception is discarded with no logging.
- The only diagnostic, `console.error("[app] Failed to stage renderer state before unload:", error)`, goes to the **main process stdout**, which is discarded for a Finder-launched app. It does not appear in `logs/main.trace.ndjson`, `logs/daemon.log`, or `log show --predicate 'process == "Orca"'`. It is only visible if you launch `Orca.app/Contents/MacOS/Orca` from a terminal.
- No toast, no dialog, no renderer console output. From the user's side the button is simply inert.

## What triggered it here

`workspaceSessionsByHostId` in `orca-data.json` held sessions for three hosts that no longer exist:

```
"ssh:<target-id-A>"      → removed; sshTargets is [], tombstone present in removedSshTargetTombstones
"ssh:<target-id-B>"      → removed; ditto
"runtime:<env-id>"       → stale runtime host session, ~9 KB
```

The renderer already knew these were stale — it logged, at hydration time:

```
[worktree-purge] hydration-time purge removing stale state for 3 worktree(s)
```

but the shutdown path still fed them to `store.stageWorkspaceSessionBeforeUnload(...)`, which merges each incoming session against the persisted state:

```js
setLocalWorkspaceSession(session, deferSnapshotFiles = false) {
  const prior = this.state.workspaceSession;
  session = sanitizeWorkspaceSessionTerminalRetirements(session, prior);
  session = pruneWorkspaceSessionBrowserHistory(pruneLocalTerminalScrollbackBuffers(session, this.state.repos));
  const normalized = normalizeWorkspaceSessionPaneIdentities(session, prior?.terminalLayoutsByTabId);
  const remappedAcknowledgements = remapAcknowledgedAgentPaneKeys(this.state.ui?.acknowledgedAgentsByPaneKey, ...);
  // ...
}
```

I was not able to capture the exact throwing line (main stdout was already lost by the time I diagnosed it), so I can only confirm the trigger data, not the precise statement.

## Workaround

Quit Orca, then delete the session state and restart:

```bash
pkill -x Orca

cd ~/Library/Application\ Support/orca/profiles/<active-profile-id>
python3 - <<'PY'
import json, pathlib
p = pathlib.Path('orca-data.json')
d = json.loads(p.read_text())
d.pop('workspaceSession', None)
d.pop('workspaceSessionsByHostId', None)
d.get('ui', {}).pop('acknowledgedAgentsByPaneKey', None)
p.write_text(json.dumps(d, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2))
PY
```

42036 → 19509 bytes; `settings`, `projects`, `repos`, `onboarding`, `automations` all preserved. After restart the red button works normally again and Orca rebuilt a clean `workspaceSession` with an empty `workspaceSessionsByHostId`. Cost: tab layout, terminal splits and browser history have to be rebuilt.

Note the file must be edited while Orca is **not** running — it rewrites `orca-data.json` continuously (I observed three rewrites within a few minutes).

## Suggested fixes

1. **Move the final `stageBeforeUnloadSync` inside the `try`.** A failure to persist the session snapshot should degrade to `{sessions: [], ui}` — exactly what the existing fallback already does — rather than making the window unclosable. Losing the tab layout is strictly better than trapping the user.

2. **Do not let a checkpoint failure veto the close.** `createShutdownCheckpointBeforeUnloadHandler` should surface the error (toast / renderer `console.error` with the real exception, instead of a bare `catch`) but still allow the unload. As written, an unrelated persistence bug is escalated into a hard UI lock-up.

3. **Make the watchdog measure progress, not delivery.** Either ack from the renderer *after* the close flow resolves rather than in the preload listener, or arm `armQuitRendererAckTimer` on every close request (not just `isQuitting`) so the non-quit path has a backstop too. Additionally, `will-prevent-unload` currently aborts the close unconditionally — for a user-initiated close that has already been confirmed, it should call `event.preventDefault()` to let the unload proceed.

Any one of the three would have turned this from "app cannot be closed, ever, with no explanation" into a recoverable, diagnosable condition.

Follow-up to #14346 and [STA-4173](https://linear.app/stably/issue/STA-4173/p1-durable-history-overlay-blocks-reattach-on-the-global-checkpoint).

## Summary

- preserve checkpoint rejection diagnostics across deadline fallback;
- add one-shot overlay, periodic, and admission breadcrumbs;
- cap non-final checkpoint work at one admission per session and four sessions globally;
- retain admission until abandoned work actually settles;
- keep final sleep/disconnect checkpoints deadline-free and lossless;
- register deterministic daemon checkpoint-isolation reliability coverage.

The scheduling policy remains in `DaemonPtyAdapter`, which owns the complete output-drain, snapshot-build, and durable-write transaction. The per-session queue remains policy-free and provides same-session serialization.

## Validation

- 208 focused checkpoint, reattach, and deep-history tests;
- Node 24 full typecheck;
- changed-code quality gate;
- 80 reliability-gate manifest checks;
- max-lines ratchet and `git diff --check`;
- broad daemon suite, relay/Electron builds, 9/9 cross-version wire checks, and 26/26 terminal-cardinality checks during internal review;
- real local Electron reattach via Playwright CDP;
- real Ubuntu 20.04 Docker SSH relay reattach with remote PID and post-reconnect command proof.

